// @run
// @log false
// @log a
// @log a
// GRADUATED from `xfail-miscompile-litunion-alias-relational-compare.vl`. This was a
// WRONG-VALUE miscompile — a valid module that ran to exit 0 and answered `x < y` with
// `true` for `x = "b"`, `y = "a"` — and the three `@log`s above are the values its own
// "WHEN IT IS FIXED" line predicted. It stays here, as a `@run` pin, because the wrong
// answer was silent in every channel: `vl check` was clean, the wasm validated, and the
// only witness was the printed value.
//
// THE MECHANISM THAT WAS WRONG, read off the disassembly of the first three lines:
//
//   type K = "a" | "b"   const x: K = "b"   const y: K = "a"   print(x < y)
//   →  (local.set $0 (i32.const 0))        ;; "b" — interned FIRST, so id 0
//      (local.set $1 (i32.const 1))        ;; "a" — interned second, id 1
//      (call $print_bool (i32.lt_s (local.get $0) (local.get $1)))
//
// A named alias `K` reps as the i32 ATOM in every scalar position (`nodeTyIsLitUnionAlias`,
// the rep #843 established), so BOTH operands arrived at `emitBinExprNode` already i32 and
// the ladder fell through to the numeric arm without ever asking whether they were atoms.
// Ids are assigned in the order the members are first INTERNED — first appearance in the
// emitted code — so `x < y` was answering "was `"b"` written before `"a"`". NOT the alias's
// member order, which is the natural guess and is wrong: `type K = "c" | "a" | "b"` sorting
// `["a", "b", "c"]` returned `a b c`, because the array literal is what interns them.
//
// THE SCOPE WAS THE WHOLE COMPILED PROGRAM, not the module, and that is what made the
// inline spelling no workaround. `"a" | "b"` written inline SOFTENS to a string ref in a
// scalar position (`ctxKeepsLitUnion` at `RC_ROOT`), so it took the string comparison and
// answered on the TEXT — but only while no same-layout ALIAS was declared ANYWHERE in the
// program graph. One unused `type K = "a" | "b"` was enough (the same-layout rule
// `literal-unions/error-same-layout-litunion-arg.vl` pins), and so was one
// `import { pathKind } from "std:fs"`, which flipped a `("file" | "dir")[]` sort from
// `dir file` to `file dir` because `std/fs.vl:130` declares `export type FileKind =
// "file" | "dir"`. That cross-module row is pinned by
// `literal-unions/litunion-order-cross-module-alias.vl`, the same-module rows by
// `literal-unions/litunion-order-is-member-text.vl`.
//
// THE FIX is one predicate and one call. `exprIsStrOrder` (`emit_classify.vl`) now asks
// `strOrderOperandIsText` — a string ref OR a literal-union ATOM — of each operand instead
// of `exprString`, and `emitStrOrder` (`wasmEmit.vl`) pushes each operand with
// `emitStrValue` instead of `emitExpr`, so an atom widens to its member string
// (`emitAtomToStr`) before the lexicographic core. The one predicate serves BOTH the
// dispatch and the `exprHasStrOp` frame reservation, so the two cannot disagree about the
// operand set.
//
// TWO THINGS DELIBERATELY DID NOT MOVE, both verified byte-identical A/B:
//   · `==`/`!=` over atoms. Equal ids iff equal texts, so the `i32.eq` in
//     `emitBinExprNode`'s litunion-equality arm is already a correct equality and a cheaper
//     one. The whole `eq-litunion-*` family is untouched.
//   · NUMERIC literal unions. `tyIsLitUnion` requires every member to be `litKind == "str"`,
//     so `type N = 1 | 2 | 3` is not a litunion to `exprIsLitAtom` at all and keeps the
//     numeric compare, which is both correct and faster
//     (`soundness/numeric-litunion-alias-membership.vl` and the grid row in
//     `literal-unions/litunion-order-is-member-text.vl`).
//
// THE COST, measured over 20,000,000 comparisons of a 5-member alias: 0.04 s before (the
// wrong `i32.lt_s`), 0.16 s after, against 0.14 s for the identical loop hand-written over
// a `string[]` — so the correct answer costs what a string comparison costs, plus the widen
// tower. Correctness first is the standing ruling; the cheaper correct designs (interning
// atom ids in global text order, or an id->rank side table so the compare stays one
// `i32.lt_s`) are FILED, not built.
import { sorted } from "std:array"

type K = "a" | "b"

const x: K = "b"
const y: K = "a"
print(x < y)

const ks: K[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(ks.sorted((p: K, q: K) => p < q)[0])

const inl: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(inl.sorted((p: "a" | "b", q: "a" | "b") => p < q)[0])
